dockchannel-hid: M4/M5 shared-memory ring transport (experimental, never booted)

Applies on top of the pinned Asahi tree (77cb8f24) plus
kernel/experiments/driver-contracts/dockchannel-hid/0001-*.patch.

MacBook Pro M5 Max (t6050) MTP firmware delivers input reports through a
shared-memory byte ring that the host registers, in addition to the
DockChannel FIFO the existing driver reads. This adds that receive path,
selected by the "apple,t6050-dockchannel-hid" compatible (or the mtp_ring
module parameter), and keeps the FIFO path's behaviour (checked by the harness).

Everything here comes from our own static analysis of the macOS drivers on
this Mac; see kernel/experiments/mtp-input/README.md for each fact and its
evidence label. Facts marked Unknown there (doorbell contents, whether the
descriptors travel through the ring, the trackpad reset path) are handled
defensively and logged, not assumed.

Changes:
  - dchid-ring.h (new): framing, checksum, ring consumer, command encoders.
  - after the first DockChannel traffic wait 100 ms, then send the awake
    report (0xc1) and register one 2 MiB device->host ring (0x91).
  - drain the ring after every DockChannel read completion (and optionally
    from a debug timer); one mutex serialises drains.
  - accept bare acknowledgements and match them by transfer id in ring mode.
  - parse the extra init blocks newer firmware appends; an "inactive" ready
    status no longer completes the interface; an interface announced without
    a HID descriptor is not created until it has one.
  - if the STM never becomes ready, create the deferred interfaces after
    3 s without its device ID (ring mode only).

Not done: trackpad firmware flow (reset goes through SMC functions on this
hardware, see README), GPIO/SMC request acknowledgement format.

Test: kernel/experiments/mtp-input/driver-test/check_driver.py

